WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.968
Why is noon sunshine so much stronger than evening sunshine, when it is the very same sun?

2
00:00:06.318 --> 00:00:12.227
And why does a skylight three times wider make a room almost nine times brighter, not three?

3
00:00:12.577 --> 00:00:17.633
Both answers come from one idea, the one at the heart of the rendering equation.

4
00:00:17.983 --> 00:00:26.111
Here is our test room. A courtyard with a round patch of bright sky overhead, and a white card on the floor measuring the light.

5
00:00:27.311 --> 00:00:30.277
First, how big is a light, as seen from the card?

6
00:00:30.627 --> 00:00:40.931
Not its size in metres, and not how many pixels it covers. What matters is how much of the sky it fills. That is its solid angle, measured in steradians.

7
00:00:41.281 --> 00:00:50.603
A whole sphere of directions is four pi steradians, about twelve point five seven. Half a sphere is two pi, about six point two eight.

8
00:00:50.953 --> 00:00:59.295
Our patch of sky is a circular cap. Its solid angle is two pi, times one minus the cosine of its angular radius.

9
00:01:00.495 --> 00:01:09.113
Now the skylight test. Same sky brightness, same camera. Only the angular radius changes, from five degrees to fifteen.

10
00:01:09.463 --> 00:01:17.655
The cap grows from zero point zero two four steradians to zero point two one four. Close to nine times more sky.

11
00:01:18.005 --> 00:01:21.312
And the card's light goes up by eight point eight two times.

12
00:01:21.662 --> 00:01:30.025
That is the skylight puzzle. Three times wider in angle means about nine times more sky, so about nine times more light.

13
00:01:31.225 --> 00:01:38.457
But why eight point eight two, and not exactly the sky ratio of eight point nine five? Because of the cosine.

14
00:01:38.807 --> 00:01:46.807
Light arriving at a slant spreads over more floor, so each direction counts in proportion to the cosine of its angle from straight up.

15
00:01:47.157 --> 00:01:52.575
Weight every bit of solid angle by that cosine, and you get the projected solid angle.

16
00:01:52.925 --> 00:01:58.941
For the cap, it is pi, times the sine of the radius, squared. Its shadow, flattened onto the floor.

17
00:01:59.291 --> 00:02:11.217
And here is the aha. The light on the card does not follow the solid angle. It follows the projected solid angle. That cosine is the whole story of noon versus evening sun.

18
00:02:12.417 --> 00:02:19.243
We tested that directly. Here is the card's light against projected solid angle, across many cap sizes.

19
00:02:19.593 --> 00:02:26.356
The measured points sit exactly on a straight line. Light equals sky brightness times projected solid angle.

20
00:02:26.706 --> 00:02:33.533
The red ghost line uses plain solid angle, forgetting the cosine, and it drifts away as the cap grows.

21
00:02:33.883 --> 00:02:45.637
For the five degree cap, projected over plain is zero point nine nine eight. At fifteen degrees, zero point nine eight three. The bigger the light, the more the cosine matters.

22
00:02:46.837 --> 00:02:52.704
Now tilt the card instead. Keep the same sky, and lean the card over by sixty degrees.

23
00:02:53.054 --> 00:02:58.046
The sky did not get darker. The card simply sees it at a slant, and catches less.

24
00:02:58.396 --> 00:03:02.876
The cosine is geometry. It is not a brightness slider you can tune.

25
00:03:03.226 --> 00:03:09.093
This is exactly why winter sun feels weak, and why solar panels are angled toward the sun.

26
00:03:10.293 --> 00:03:14.474
One more consequence. A whole hemisphere is two pi steradians.

27
00:03:14.824 --> 00:03:19.027
But weight it by the cosine, and the projected hemisphere is just pi.

28
00:03:19.377 --> 00:03:29.937
So a uniform sky of brightness L puts L times pi on a flat card, not L times two pi. That pi, in every Lambert formula, comes from here.

29
00:03:31.137 --> 00:03:35.595
Finally, a path tracer estimates this integral by random directions.

30
00:03:35.945 --> 00:03:46.655
Sampling evenly over solid angle, or in proportion to the cosine, both converge, as long as each sample is divided by the probability it was drawn with.

31
00:03:47.005 --> 00:03:52.999
From sixteen to one thousand twenty-four samples, the noise melts away, and the error falls.

32
00:03:54.199 --> 00:03:55.799
Now the honest limits.

33
00:03:56.149 --> 00:04:00.843
One courtyard, one round sky cap, one matte card, and a software renderer.

34
00:04:01.193 --> 00:04:10.259
Every number comes from the exact formulas and the estimator, not from the pictures. The run finishes with twenty-eight checks passed and none failed.

35
00:04:11.459 --> 00:04:12.803
So, the takeaway.

36
00:04:13.153 --> 00:04:17.420
Size of a light means solid angle, not metres, and not pixels.

37
00:04:17.770 --> 00:04:23.338
Surfaces respond to projected solid angle, the solid angle weighted by the cosine.

38
00:04:23.688 --> 00:04:31.923
That is the cosine inside the rendering equation, written down by James Kajiya in nineteen eighty-six. Thanks for watching.
